# Session Transition: Documentation and Handoff (Go + Gin)

## Purpose
Ensure proper documentation and knowledge transfer between sessions. Each session completion
generates a summary that enables efficient continuation in the next session.

## Session Completion Triggers

Transition to the next session when:
- All E2E tests passing for the current endpoint (`go test ./... -v`)
- Code quality checks pass (`gofmt -l .` empty, `golangci-lint run` clean)
- Implementation complete per session requirements
- No known bugs or issues
- Session documentation updated
- Session plan objectives met

## Session Summary Generation

Create `plan/sessions/session-N-summary.md` for each completed session.

### Session Summary Template Structure

```markdown
# Session N Summary: Phase M ([HTTP Method] [Endpoint Path])

## Overview
Brief description of what was implemented in this session.

## Implementation Details

### Database Changes
- Tables created or modified
- Migration applied (golang-migrate file or GORM AutoMigrate)
- Indexes added

### Models Created
- GORM model (with file path)
- API DTOs (with file path)
- Purpose of each

### Repository Methods
- Methods implemented
- File location

### Service Layer
- Services created or updated
- Business logic implemented
- File location

### Handler
- Endpoint path and method
- Request/response DTOs
- Dependency-injection factory signature

## Tests

### E2E Tests
- Test files created
- Test scenarios covered
- All tests passing: YES/NO

### Unit Tests
- Services tested (mocked repository)
- Coverage (if measured: `go test ./... -cover`)

## Shared Libraries

### Available for Next Phases
- Repository types that can be reused
- Service methods available
- Models representing core entities
- Utilities or helpers created

### Dependencies
- External packages added to go.mod (`go get ...@vX.Y.Z`)
- Configuration variables added to .env

## Known Limitations

### Current Implementation
- Features not implemented yet
- Performance considerations
- Edge cases not handled

### Technical Debt
- Code that needs refactoring
- Tests that need improvement
- Documentation gaps

## Next Session Recommendations

### Suggested Next Session
Recommend the next session based on:
- Dependency on the current session
- Complexity progression
- User value
- Session plan availability

### Prerequisite Work
- Additional models needed
- Migrations required
- Shared services to build

## Learnings and Gotchas

### What Worked Well
- Patterns that were effective
- Tools that helped
- Design decisions that paid off

### Challenges Encountered
- Problems solved during implementation
- Workarounds applied (e.g. GORM quirks, Gin binding edge cases)
- Areas needing improvement

### Time Estimates
- Estimated time for phase: X minutes
- Actual time spent: Y minutes
- Variance explanation

## Code Quality Metrics

### Formatting
- `gofmt -l .` clean: YES/NO
- Doc comments on new exports: YES/NO
- `golangci-lint run` clean: YES/NO

### Testing
- E2E test coverage: X scenarios
- Unit test coverage: Y%
- All tests passing: YES/NO

## Files Changed

### Created
- List of new files with paths

### Modified
- List of modified files with paths

### Deleted
- List of deleted files (if any)
```

## Example Session Summary

```markdown
# Session 2 Summary: Phase 1 (POST /posts)

## Overview
Implemented blog post creation endpoint with full GORM/PostgreSQL integration and E2E testing.

## Implementation Details

### Database Changes
- Created posts table via golang-migrate: migrations/000002_create_posts_table.up.sql
- Added index on author_id
- Applied migration successfully to local database

### Models Created
- Post (internal/model/post.go): GORM model, DB layer
- CreatePostRequest (internal/model/post.go): API request DTO (camelCase JSON)
- PostResponse (internal/model/post.go): API response DTO
- ToPostResponse() converter function

### Repository Methods
- GormPostRepository.Create() — create new post
- File: internal/repository/post_repository.go

### Service Layer
- PostService.CreatePost() — business logic for post creation
- Includes error wrapping and structured logging
- File: internal/service/post_service.go

### Handler
- POST /api/posts
- Handler: handler.CreatePost(svc *service.PostService) gin.HandlerFunc
- Request: CreatePostRequest (title, content, authorId)
- Response: PostResponse (postId, title, content, authorId, createdAt)
- File: internal/handler/posts.go

## Tests

### E2E Tests
- internal/handler/posts_test.go::TestCreatePost_Success
- internal/handler/posts_test.go::TestCreatePost_ValidationError
- All tests passing: YES

### Unit Tests
- internal/service/post_service_test.go — service layer with mocked repository
- Coverage: 95% of service methods

## Shared Libraries

### Available for Next Sessions
- GormPostRepository — can be extended with GetByID() in Session 3
- PostService — can be extended with GetPostByID()
- Post model — represents the core post entity

### Dependencies
- No new packages added
- No new environment variables needed

## Known Limitations

### Current Implementation
- Only creation implemented, no read/update/delete yet
- No support for tags or categories
- No content length validation beyond binding tags
- No duplicate post prevention

### Technical Debt
- Consider adding content sanitization
- May need a post status field (draft/published)
- Service layer could accept a logger via constructor for better testability

## Next Phase Recommendations

### Suggested Next Session
Session 3: Phase 2 (GET /posts/{id})
- Depends on Session 2 (need posts to read)
- Simple implementation (reuse GormPostRepository)
- High user value (view created posts)
- Session plan: plan/sessions/session-3-phase-2.md

### Prerequisite Work
- Add GormPostRepository.GetByID() method
- Add PostService.GetPostByID() method
- Add GET handler to internal/handler/posts.go
- Write E2E test for retrieval

## Learnings and Gotchas

### What Worked Well
- Layered model architecture (GORM model vs DTOs) prevented JSON leakage of DB fields
- Interface-at-point-of-use made the service trivially mockable in unit tests
- TDD approach caught a binding-tag typo early
- Factory-function handlers made dependency injection explicit and testable

### Challenges Encountered
- Initial confusion with `gorm:"type:uuid;default:gen_random_uuid()"` needing the `pgcrypto`
  extension enabled in Postgres
- Needed `c.Request.Context()` (not `context.Background()`) to propagate cancellation
- Test fixtures required unique author IDs per test run

### Time Estimates
- Estimated time: 25 minutes
- Actual time: 30 minutes
- Variance: +5 minutes debugging the UUID default extension

## Code Quality Metrics

### Formatting
- `gofmt -l .` clean: YES
- Doc comments on new exports: YES
- `golangci-lint run` clean: YES

### Testing
- E2E test coverage: 2 scenarios
- Unit test coverage: 95%
- All tests passing: YES

## Files Changed

### Created
- internal/model/post.go
- internal/repository/post_repository.go
- internal/service/post_service.go
- internal/service/post_service_test.go
- internal/handler/posts.go
- internal/handler/posts_test.go
- migrations/000002_create_posts_table.up.sql
- migrations/000002_create_posts_table.down.sql

### Modified
- cmd/server/main.go (wired posts routes)
- go.mod / go.sum (no new deps this session)
- .env.example (no changes)

### Deleted
- None
```

## Session Transition Checklist

Before generating the summary and moving to the next session:

### Code Quality
- [ ] All E2E tests passing
- [ ] All unit tests passing
- [ ] `gofmt -l .` clean
- [ ] Doc comments on all new exported identifiers
- [ ] `golangci-lint run` clean
- [ ] `go vet ./...` clean

### Documentation
- [ ] Code comments for complex logic
- [ ] README updated if needed
- [ ] OpenAPI spec updated if the contract changed
- [ ] Migration files documented

### Testing
- [ ] E2E test for the success case
- [ ] E2E test for error cases
- [ ] E2E test for validation
- [ ] Unit tests for complex business logic
- [ ] Test coverage adequate

### Implementation
- [ ] Feature complete per session requirements
- [ ] Error handling implemented (wrapped errors)
- [ ] Structured logging added
- [ ] No hardcoded values
- [ ] Secrets properly managed

### Knowledge Transfer
- [ ] Shared libraries documented
- [ ] Dependencies documented
- [ ] Known limitations listed
- [ ] Next session recommendations provided
- [ ] Session plan objectives met

## Generating the Session Summary

When the user says "Session N complete" or "Transition to next session":

1. Read the current session's implementation
2. Analyze all changes made
3. Generate a comprehensive summary
4. Save to `plan/sessions/session-N-summary.md`
5. Provide a brief verbal summary to the user
6. Ask for confirmation before proceeding

## Session Kickoff

When starting a new session:

1. Read previous session summaries
2. Review the next session plan (`plan/sessions/session-N-phase-M.md`)
3. Identify available shared libraries (repositories/services already built)
4. Check dependencies
5. Confirm session requirements with the user
6. Begin with E2E test writing

## Research Export

When encountering issues requiring external research:

Generate `research-export.md` using `templates/research-export.md`:
- Document the problem clearly
- List all attempted solutions
- Include relevant Go code snippets and `go vet`/compiler output
- Formulate specific questions
- Provide context from phase summaries

## Continuous Improvement

After each session:
- Update time estimates based on actual time
- Note patterns that worked well
- Document challenges for future reference (e.g. GORM edge cases)
- Suggest process improvements
- Update session plans for future projects

## Session Transition Anti-Patterns

Do NOT:
- Move to the next session with failing tests
- Skip session summary generation
- Document only what was built (also document why)
- Forget to note known limitations
- Skip recommendations for the next session
- Generate a summary without reading the code
- Use generic summaries (be specific)
- Forget to document shared libraries
- Skip session plan review
- Automatically start the next session
- Combine multiple sessions into one
